00:17:58,542 --> 00:18:04,305
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't be, you know, doing the melting.

174
00:18:05,207 --> 00:18:09,149
[SPEAKER_03]: when there is still gravel and dirt in the gold.

175
00:18:09,689 --> 00:18:20,514
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why it's like the Buddha wouldn't be talking to people about... Yeah, so, you know, two years from now, when you have developed your virtue and your sense of change, you're going to be doing this and this.

176
00:18:21,134 --> 00:18:22,935
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, precisely right now,

177
00:18:24,208 --> 00:18:28,389
[SPEAKER_03]: You develop that timeless principle, starting with your virtue.

178
00:18:28,489 --> 00:18:34,811
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't... you're not waiting for something else to happen, in order for you to be able to keep the precepts.

179
00:18:34,871 --> 00:18:43,314
[SPEAKER_03]: You realize, right now, at this moment, there is an opportunity to break this precept, and at the same time, there is a choice to not do it.

180
00:18:45,715 --> 00:18:49,956
[SPEAKER_03]: And, eh... that's already developing the principle of Bhattishthana Upādā, what this is.

181
00:18:49,976 --> 00:18:50,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

182
00:18:50,436 --> 00:18:53,577
[SPEAKER_01]: And, like, the also important point there is, like, the...

183
00:18:54,014 --> 00:19:04,804
[SPEAKER_01]: What that kind of refraining from doing is, what that is kind of opening up, is the recognition that there is... that it is a choice to break the precept.

184
00:19:04,824 --> 00:19:13,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any... like breaking any precept, or acting out of any pressure, what that has in common in whatever kind of situation, is that it feels like

185
00:19:14,206 --> 00:19:14,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Compulsion.

186
00:19:14,946 --> 00:19:17,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Feels like I'm under a kind of a compulsion.

187
00:19:17,947 --> 00:19:19,367
[SPEAKER_01]: I must, I must do this.

188
00:19:19,867 --> 00:19:29,790
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the problem that can also happen is that you can, well, you probably would initially then just be kind of replacing it with another sort of, okay, I must just sort of not do this.

189
00:19:29,910 --> 00:19:31,630
[SPEAKER_01]: I must sort of do something else.

190
00:19:32,431 --> 00:19:40,673
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you just kind of don't allow this one to happen to that extent, it's it's already kind of

191
00:19:44,612 --> 00:19:53,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, just cancelling out that layer of... of the defilement that was causing you to do it.

192
00:19:53,900 --> 00:19:58,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So, allowing you to kind of remove that layer that you get to kind of see another one.

193
00:19:58,663 --> 00:20:07,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And that kind of carries all the way through, because ultimately, what you can kind of realize is like that sort of sense of like, you know, I'm under pressure to...

194
00:20:07,671 --> 00:20:09,852
[SPEAKER_01]: I must tell a lie in this situation.

195
00:20:09,912 --> 00:20:14,156
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't... this person just can't know the truth or whatever.

196
00:20:15,378 --> 00:20:28,241
[SPEAKER_01]: And what you can ultimately realize is that it's your craving and nothing else that is the source of that kind of compulsion.

197
00:20:28,841 --> 00:20:39,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you were to be able to stop craving in that situation, to stop resisting the unpleasantness of the idea of this person knowing the truth or whatever,

198
00:20:40,710 --> 00:20:42,091
[SPEAKER_01]: You would have literally no reason.

199
00:20:42,332 --> 00:20:45,435
[SPEAKER_01]: There would be no reason to need to tell a lie.

200
00:20:45,535 --> 00:20:48,317
[SPEAKER_01]: You would just be free, basically.

201
00:20:48,518 --> 00:20:56,405
[SPEAKER_00]: But at that layer, what would be the most acute way of not resisting the prospect of telling the truth?

202
00:20:58,307 --> 00:21:01,110
[SPEAKER_01]: What would be the most acute way of not resisting?

203
00:21:01,670 --> 00:21:03,032
[SPEAKER_00]: The prospect of telling the truth.

204
00:21:06,506 --> 00:21:15,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, like recognizing the unpleasantness of the idea of it, and basically being patient in regard to it.

205
00:21:15,253 --> 00:21:18,415
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yes, that would be a more subtle recognition.

206
00:21:19,078 --> 00:21:26,181
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, at that point, the most acute way to not resist would be to simply just... Yeah, well, that's kind of what I meant.

207
00:21:26,221 --> 00:21:32,443
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you can carry that principle up all the way through, but at that point, like, if somebody really is feeling that compulsion, you're not going to recognize it.

208
00:21:32,463 --> 00:21:32,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

209
00:21:32,783 --> 00:21:36,485
[SPEAKER_03]: So, because all of these feelings and like, you know, the...

210
00:21:37,565 --> 00:21:46,447
[SPEAKER_03]: Hindrances and all that stuff would become apparent once you have not been constricted on that level.

211
00:21:46,587 --> 00:21:46,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

212
00:21:47,287 --> 00:21:48,668
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I meant.

213
00:21:48,988 --> 00:21:50,968
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is still following the same principle.

214
00:21:51,028 --> 00:21:59,510
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just like not perpetuating that level of it is making the subtler levels clear.

215
00:22:02,417 --> 00:22:08,162
[SPEAKER_04]: If you're in the habit of lying habitually, you're not going to see the pressure to lie.

216
00:22:09,223 --> 00:22:12,225
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not going to see the mental kind of background to it.

217
00:22:12,245 --> 00:22:15,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you'll start feeling the pressure, but once you decide to not do it.

218
00:22:15,768 --> 00:22:16,809
[SPEAKER_04]: Right, yeah.

219
00:22:16,969 --> 00:22:27,278
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it shows you that that's kind of why speech needs to be before... like, you need to purify your speech before you can purify your mind.

220
00:22:27,338 --> 00:22:27,859
[SPEAKER_04]: Just because...

221
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,200
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, that's kind of the layering there.

222
00:22:32,260 --> 00:22:45,165
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and the important point is, you have to find, and it could take some investigation, like, what stage or what layer are you actually in?

223
00:22:45,185 --> 00:22:48,626
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is the thing that you slip on or in regard to?

224
00:22:48,926 --> 00:22:50,407
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you see that.

225
00:22:50,887 --> 00:22:52,768
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the stuff you need to worry about.

226
00:22:53,228 --> 00:22:53,408
[SPEAKER_03]: Like,

227
00:22:56,138 --> 00:23:07,706
[SPEAKER_03]: At the level of a person who isn't able to keep their precepts, if they start worrying about not resisting the feeling, that's going to be actually distracting them from what they actually can do right now.

228
00:23:07,886 --> 00:23:13,570
[SPEAKER_03]: And factually, like, cut off an entire, like, without remainder, that layer is cut off.

229
00:23:14,271 --> 00:23:15,652
[SPEAKER_03]: That layer of defilement is cut off.

230
00:23:17,682 --> 00:23:23,465
[SPEAKER_03]: So, noticing what is the actual compulsion here.

231
00:23:23,605 --> 00:23:29,048
[SPEAKER_03]: You might be, you know, the opposite principle applies as well.

232
00:23:29,548 --> 00:23:37,592
[SPEAKER_03]: If you, let's say, underestimate the subtlety of the compulsion, and you think it's just about dealing with the actions,

233
00:23:38,252 --> 00:23:53,410
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas, really, the stuff that's propelling you there is your mental craving now to overly resolve the situation, whereas you're at... you're already restrained, but you're kind of like flogging a dead horse, because you want now, like...

234
00:23:53,490 --> 00:23:56,234
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't want to feel the pressure to do these actions anymore, even.

235
00:23:56,930 --> 00:24:08,958
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's where you would be overstepping the mark, and not seeing that actually, now, you should be working on a more subtle level, of not, now, on this mental level, revolving around that restlessness.

236
00:24:10,459 --> 00:24:11,499
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

237
00:24:12,360 --> 00:24:17,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's where... wherever there's pressure, essentially, to act.

238
00:24:17,463 --> 00:24:18,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

239
00:24:19,004 --> 00:24:19,585
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's...

240
00:24:21,386 --> 00:24:33,997
[SPEAKER_03]: That internal, you could say, that internal passion that the sutta talks about, you could measure that by saying, not like what do I feel or what is there, but do I want to act?

241
00:24:35,118 --> 00:24:36,419
[SPEAKER_03]: Is there a plan?

242
00:24:36,459 --> 00:24:40,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Do I have intentions to act out of this in the future?

243
00:24:41,583 --> 00:24:46,928
[SPEAKER_03]: If that's not there, that's not an internal passion that you have at that moment.

244
00:24:48,435 --> 00:24:50,778
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's just basically a feeling.

245
00:24:51,639 --> 00:25:04,493
[SPEAKER_02]: If truly, there is no plan to give in to it, no matter how big the wave is, if there's no plan to, you know, jump into the ocean or run away, then...

246
00:25:05,990 --> 00:25:07,971
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no problem there anymore.

247
00:25:08,171 --> 00:25:10,993
[SPEAKER_02]: But the problem would be sustaining that.

248
00:25:11,593 --> 00:25:14,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's so easy to just fall into one of the other extremes.

249
00:25:15,175 --> 00:25:18,937
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, that's the natural tendency.

250
00:25:20,836 --> 00:25:23,479
[SPEAKER_02]: The other thing I wanted to mention, so that's...

251
00:25:23,499 --> 00:25:36,154
[SPEAKER_02]: I already sort of brought up for a second, but the padisamupāda, or dependent origination, is usually thought of as this sequence of cause and effect.

252
00:25:37,035 --> 00:25:38,757
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's actually quite

253
00:25:40,568 --> 00:25:45,692
[SPEAKER_03]: That view is obscuring this responsibility that we're talking about.

254
00:25:45,752 --> 00:25:49,815
[SPEAKER_03]: The sense of... not just responsibility in a bad sense, but in a good sense as well.

255
00:25:49,875 --> 00:25:59,322
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if you don't recognize, actually, it's on you, well, that does make the practice a lot more frustrating than it should be.

256
00:26:00,483 --> 00:26:02,145
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the...

257
00:26:03,610 --> 00:26:13,313
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not a cause and effect in the sense that I did something in the past and now this craving is like has to be here because I did something in the past.

258
00:26:13,553 --> 00:26:14,273
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a wrong view.

259
00:26:15,934 --> 00:26:19,235
[SPEAKER_02]: The craving is there if there is ignorance now.

260
00:26:19,875 --> 00:26:21,556
[SPEAKER_02]: And you have it, you have it say in that.

261
00:26:21,576 --> 00:26:29,178
[SPEAKER_02]: Like you can, first of all, recognize yourself acting out and then restrain those activities.

262
00:26:30,570 --> 00:26:31,791
[SPEAKER_02]: That you're doing on purpose.

263
00:26:32,571 --> 00:26:33,291
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, not on purpose.

264
00:26:33,331 --> 00:26:34,692
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's say, eh...

265
00:26:34,852 --> 00:26:35,913
[SPEAKER_02]: It does involve intention.

266
00:26:35,953 --> 00:26:37,233
[SPEAKER_02]: It never happens by accident.

267
00:26:37,874 --> 00:26:42,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

268
00:26:42,316 --> 00:26:44,017
[SPEAKER_00]: But īsaṃupāda really should be understood.

269
00:26:44,237 --> 00:26:54,522
[SPEAKER_03]: It is the epitome of that timeless nature in the sense that craving is there not because craving has to be there.

270
00:26:54,983 --> 00:26:56,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is there because of ignorance.

271
00:26:58,324 --> 00:26:59,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it's like...

272
00:26:59,784 --> 00:27:05,789
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, practically speaking, you could say it's there when it's not known.

273
00:27:06,329 --> 00:27:07,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

274
00:27:07,430 --> 00:27:08,431
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

275
00:27:09,111 --> 00:27:16,557
[SPEAKER_02]: You could say craving is there when craving is not known, or craving is there when feeling is not known, or craving is there when form is not known.

276
00:27:16,657 --> 00:27:18,879
[SPEAKER_03]: Any of these aspects you could take.

277
00:27:18,939 --> 00:27:28,746
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, yeah, basically, like, you, if you think, okay, I'm supposed to resolve this somehow.

278
00:27:29,639 --> 00:27:32,099
[SPEAKER_02]: That will prevent you from seeing padi-saṃpāda.

279
00:27:32,259 --> 00:27:34,600
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not supposed to resolve it.

280
00:27:34,620 --> 00:27:36,420
[SPEAKER_02]: You're supposed to see with this, this is.

281
00:27:37,440 --> 00:27:43,962
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you basically suspend your preconceived notions of like how much of it should be there and how much of it shouldn't be there.

282
00:27:44,922 --> 00:27:45,062
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

283
00:27:45,102 --> 00:27:48,422
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is I sustain the perspective that with this, this is.

284
00:27:49,022 --> 00:27:54,143
[SPEAKER_03]: Like with this body, that's here, subject to aging, sickness, death.

285
00:27:55,224 --> 00:27:57,864
[SPEAKER_03]: While that is there, these thoughts are happening.

286
00:27:59,181 --> 00:28:17,930
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, if you maintain that, sort of, two sides of the coin, and you don't fall away from it, you absolutely don't need to worry about, like, you know, how much thinking is too much thinking, or, you know, how long will this last.

287
00:28:18,030 --> 00:28:18,951
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter anymore.

288
00:28:19,231 --> 00:28:21,652
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is to sustain that perspective.

289
00:28:23,044 --> 00:28:25,307
[SPEAKER_00]: This would be going against the stream.

290
00:28:25,807 --> 00:28:25,987
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

291
00:28:26,928 --> 00:28:34,016
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what... that is the only really thing that you can do to prevent yourself from acting out in the future, is like right now.

292
00:28:34,942 --> 00:28:36,943
[SPEAKER_02]: The stuff that's pressuring you to act, don't do it.

293
00:28:37,504 --> 00:28:44,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like, you know, rearrange the circumstances so that in the future, you know, when it comes up, you have like the perfect plan to deal with it.

294
00:28:44,869 --> 00:28:45,649
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not how it works.

295
00:28:45,829 --> 00:28:51,873
[SPEAKER_02]: It's right now, when you have the inclination to act out of something seemingly unrelated.

296
00:28:52,274 --> 00:29:03,321
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you know, the problem is that you tend to give in to lying, as we said, and then now you have the compulsion to, you know, I don't know, go and watch TV when

297
00:29:05,482 --> 00:29:08,103
[SPEAKER_02]: It's currently rooted in distraction and it's seemingly unrelated.

298
00:29:08,123 --> 00:29:18,287
[SPEAKER_03]: You would think, well, you know, if I just come up with the perfect sort of psychotherapeutic step-by-step plan to never lie, that's how I won't.

299
00:29:18,327 --> 00:29:18,627
[SPEAKER_03]: But no.

300
00:29:19,287 --> 00:29:24,469
[SPEAKER_03]: You stop doing these things by ceasing to act out of pressure in general.

301
00:29:24,909 --> 00:29:26,609
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's... that's another thing.

302
00:29:26,629 --> 00:29:28,750
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is defined in the suttas.

303
00:29:28,970 --> 00:29:34,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, usually, you would tend to assume that craving is some specific... let's say I'm craving for this.

304
00:29:35,405 --> 00:29:47,670
[SPEAKER_03]: But actually, the way craving is defined in the suttas is like the craving that leads to repeated being, the lighting here and there, or now here and now there.

305
00:29:48,070 --> 00:29:50,831
[SPEAKER_03]: Meaning, it's never about the specific thing.

306
00:29:50,871 --> 00:29:58,814
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just a very general tendency to lean in either towards or against.

307
00:29:59,790 --> 00:30:07,637
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it literally doesn't matter whether it's towards lying, whether it's towards sensuality, whether it's towards acting out of aversion.

308
00:30:08,898 --> 00:30:11,100
[SPEAKER_03]: All of that is exactly the same craving.

309
00:30:11,501 --> 00:30:22,051
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why you will see, like, if you're acting out of sensuality for a while, you've been proliferating that, and then something comes up that before wouldn't have irritated you as much.

310
00:30:23,005 --> 00:30:32,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, it's going to irritate you, because it's the same craving that's growing, even though the content of the defilements, so to speak, is different.

311
00:30:33,814 --> 00:30:35,435
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's...

312
00:30:37,076 --> 00:30:43,842
[SPEAKER_02]: Fundamentally, it has to be... it has to start with body and speech, like restraining these things.

313
00:30:44,482 --> 00:30:50,988
[SPEAKER_03]: Practically, you will be much closer to really seeing Patissima Upāda by restraining yourself in that way,

314
00:30:52,325 --> 00:30:56,308
[SPEAKER_03]: Again, by seeing that... well, the choice.

315
00:30:56,588 --> 00:31:00,230
[SPEAKER_03]: Recognizing this is a choice that I can simply not make.

316
00:31:00,250 --> 00:31:10,217
[SPEAKER_02]: That's closer, much closer to seeing patisampada than just, you know, writing these charts that people like to fiddle around with and all that.

317
00:31:11,317 --> 00:31:13,359
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted to say, I think...

318
00:31:13,379 --> 00:31:16,941
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that point is quite important though, because it can easily...

319
00:31:17,061 --> 00:31:20,083
[SPEAKER_01]: I just want to emphasize it, because it might be easy to overlook.

320
00:31:20,830 --> 00:31:45,982
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is something that can kind of get in the way, the fact that it's really just like... the way to do it correctly is to simply not do it, without making that into something else that you're now, like, doing, or kind of thinking that in order to not do things, like, because that's quite a common thing as well, or something that I used to think, like, in order to not do these things, I must, I don't know,

321
00:31:46,493 --> 00:31:55,875
[SPEAKER_01]: practice a specific type of mindfulness or be very aware of like every bodily action or have a sort of a practice that will allow me to not do these things.

322
00:31:56,336 --> 00:32:07,919
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's literally just on the level of when there's pressure, I don't act out of it to the extent I'm able to see the pressure and to the extent I'm able to kind of reveal my responsibility in it.

323
00:32:08,479 --> 00:32:09,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, if I can see, okay,

324
00:32:10,098 --> 00:32:12,979
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a pressure, too, even just on the level of breaking the precepts.

325
00:32:13,059 --> 00:32:15,100
[SPEAKER_01]: But I've decided I'm not going to break the precepts.

326
00:32:15,520 --> 00:32:16,761
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm pressured to break them.

327
00:32:17,261 --> 00:32:18,302
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm not going to do it.

328
00:32:18,342 --> 00:32:20,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to that extent, I can see I have a choice.

329
00:32:21,283 --> 00:32:22,544
[SPEAKER_01]: And... and that's it.

330
00:32:22,764 --> 00:32:23,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I leave it there.

331
00:32:23,964 --> 00:32:32,528
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm not, kind of, building anything else on top of it, or doing anything, or seeing it as another... as another, kind of, eh...

332
00:32:33,414 --> 00:32:34,576
[SPEAKER_02]: Turning that into a new activity.

333
00:32:34,596 --> 00:32:36,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Turning that into a new activity, yeah.

334
00:32:36,619 --> 00:32:41,026
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where it can often... because that is exactly the pressure.

335
00:32:41,066 --> 00:32:44,572
[SPEAKER_01]: It's to make this into a new thing to rely on.

336
00:32:44,652 --> 00:32:45,714
[SPEAKER_03]: And why would you do that?

337
00:32:47,184 --> 00:32:52,507
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, because basically, like, the thing is, you're still not taking responsibility.

338
00:32:52,687 --> 00:33:05,655
[SPEAKER_03]: When that happens, it's like, you're basically, implicitly, you obviously, you're not telling that to yourself, but the idea is, no, I have the intention to act because this thing is here, and therefore, I need to get rid of it so that I won't act out.

339
00:33:06,295 --> 00:33:08,217
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, it's you choose to act out.

340
00:33:08,598 --> 00:33:16,826
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't matter how, like, even if it's like right flying in front of your eyes, it's you who chooses to give into it every single time.

341
00:33:17,507 --> 00:33:20,611
[SPEAKER_01]: So then... And that's the same.

342
00:33:20,891 --> 00:33:27,378
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would be then the same reason that you would be failing to... that would prevent you, no matter how far you take this.

343
00:33:27,774 --> 00:33:30,655
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that same reason that would prevent you from seeing your own craving.

344
