513
00:49:18,037 --> 00:49:18,837
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, like,

514
00:49:19,526 --> 00:49:38,452
[SPEAKER_01]: The person who, upon recognizing there is passion for sights internally, immediately switches it to there is no passion for sights internally, because that passion cannot exist in the environment of knowledge, where there's no ignorance.

515
00:49:38,532 --> 00:49:42,753
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like knowing it is the abandonment of it.

516
00:49:43,909 --> 00:49:44,610
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the...

517
00:49:44,970 --> 00:49:50,174
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, I've mentioned it a few times already, but the thing is, it's probably easily overlooked.

518
00:49:51,936 --> 00:49:53,937
[SPEAKER_02]: That's one of those things that clicks only later.

519
00:49:54,798 --> 00:49:56,479
[SPEAKER_02]: You've heard it a million times.

520
00:49:56,499 --> 00:49:56,719
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

521
00:49:58,020 --> 00:50:00,162
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is not there by itself.

522
00:50:00,242 --> 00:50:02,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is only there when there is ignorance.

523
00:50:03,084 --> 00:50:05,146
[SPEAKER_02]: And every Buddhist would say, oh, yeah, of course.

524
00:50:05,758 --> 00:50:15,101
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, like, factually, when you're trying to get rid of stuff, it's the usual way of practicing, you carry the implication that the craving is sort of like primordial, and it's there on its own.

525
00:50:16,402 --> 00:50:22,424
[SPEAKER_03]: But actually, no, like, the craving is... the craving is there to the extent that ignorance is there.

526
00:50:22,964 --> 00:50:27,326
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you overlook, if you don't put things in the right order, basically,

527
00:50:29,184 --> 00:50:34,408
[SPEAKER_02]: So, really, you're going to be dealing with the symptoms of craving.

528
00:50:34,428 --> 00:50:37,170
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter how much you think you're dealing with the actual craving.

529
00:50:37,891 --> 00:50:42,875
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's a difficult thing.

530
00:50:44,416 --> 00:50:49,860
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't give that immediate satisfaction that you expect.

531
00:50:56,355 --> 00:51:09,320
[SPEAKER_01]: One way to understand that practically, or at least to kind of get an idea of it, is that, like, if you think of craving as... as a thing, something else that I have to deal with, it's not... that's... that's not going to be it.

532
00:51:09,340 --> 00:51:10,560
[SPEAKER_01]: That's going to be a symptom of it.

533
00:51:10,880 --> 00:51:14,461
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically the closest thing you can find to your point of view.

534
00:51:14,701 --> 00:51:15,682
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right where you are.

535
00:51:16,062 --> 00:51:18,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what... what's... what's the closest thing to you?

536
00:51:21,825 --> 00:51:28,330
[SPEAKER_02]: And that can only be revealed not by trying to figure it out, but by... if you have removed these more subtle layers.

537
00:51:28,370 --> 00:51:32,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, there's always going to be a layer that you can see, as I've repeated multiple times.

538
00:51:32,773 --> 00:51:34,915
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the layer where you work on.

539
00:51:35,336 --> 00:51:38,979
[SPEAKER_02]: Removing that layer is what's going to make the next layer apparent.

540
00:51:39,399 --> 00:51:41,080
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, removing that layer is going to make...

541
00:51:41,140 --> 00:51:46,525
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if you're here and then you're trying to figure it out, like, okay, so, what is it that, you know, is preventing...

542
00:51:48,026 --> 00:51:51,047
[SPEAKER_02]: Anāgāmī, from becoming arahant, and then you're like down here.

543
00:51:51,447 --> 00:51:52,108
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not gonna work.

544
00:51:52,148 --> 00:52:04,934
[SPEAKER_02]: So... and then the... related to what you said about craving, that's the... yeah, that's exactly what I just mentioned, that it's... it delights here and there.

545
00:52:06,955 --> 00:52:08,396
[SPEAKER_03]: It has nothing to do with what it is.

546
00:52:09,056 --> 00:52:13,159
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the principle of, like, taking things up wherever it can.

547
00:52:14,251 --> 00:52:22,196
[SPEAKER_03]: So, for as long as you think that the craving is this object, by definition, you're not seeing craving, or this feeling, or whatever.

548
00:52:23,136 --> 00:52:25,477
[SPEAKER_02]: It simply cannot be something specific.

549
00:52:27,418 --> 00:52:37,644
[SPEAKER_02]: So, all of that is like, you know, would be recognized by first, as we talked about before, seeing the signs of the mind, and learning what yonisoni-sakāra is, and so on.

550
00:52:38,285 --> 00:52:41,586
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, so, actually, the sutta...

551
00:52:43,037 --> 00:52:51,006
[SPEAKER_02]: It's good that you brought that up, because it does sound, and the first time I read it, I do remember, I thought like, okay, yeah, this is talking about two different people or something.

552
00:52:51,546 --> 00:52:55,170
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, like, the sutta itself is saying the dhamma is timeless.

553
00:52:55,270 --> 00:52:55,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

554
00:52:56,191 --> 00:52:59,395
[SPEAKER_03]: And is using that to explain how the dhamma is timeless.

555
00:52:59,975 --> 00:53:00,896
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's basically saying

556
00:53:02,148 --> 00:53:09,994
[SPEAKER_03]: Without any time involved, a person can go from there being passion internally to there being no passion internally.

557
00:53:10,494 --> 00:53:17,079
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's not because... it's not that they couldn't do it because they haven't been practicing long enough.

558
00:53:17,940 --> 00:53:18,981
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not the primary cause.

559
00:53:19,241 --> 00:53:21,063
[SPEAKER_03]: They couldn't do it because they didn't know how to do it.

560
00:53:21,823 --> 00:53:25,506
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's why, like, you know, from the very beginning, everyone

561
00:53:26,166 --> 00:53:28,926
[SPEAKER_02]: It can be free from greed, aversion and delusion.

562
00:53:28,946 --> 00:53:35,968
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing blocking that magical energy that you clear out through practice.

563
00:53:36,128 --> 00:53:39,928
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing that's blocking it is greed, aversion and delusion.

564
00:53:41,309 --> 00:53:42,609
[SPEAKER_02]: And it feeds itself, obviously.

565
00:53:42,629 --> 00:53:44,449
[SPEAKER_02]: So, greed, aversion and delusion feeds ignorance.

566
00:53:44,649 --> 00:53:46,390
[SPEAKER_02]: Ignorance feeds greed, aversion and delusion.

567
00:53:47,990 --> 00:53:54,591
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, you could be an arahant today, if you just understand fully how not to have passion internally.

568
00:53:55,444 --> 00:54:04,151
[SPEAKER_03]: That there's nothing like... that there is no reason why you have to... why time has to pass.

569
00:54:04,431 --> 00:54:05,352
[SPEAKER_03]: Practically, it will.

570
00:54:06,032 --> 00:54:08,694
[SPEAKER_03]: So, don't be expecting like I can get enlightened overnight.

571
00:54:09,395 --> 00:54:16,861
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if you understand it that way, you will actually see it is always fully your responsibility.

572
00:54:18,100 --> 00:54:18,660
[SPEAKER_03]: Nothing can come.

573
00:54:18,820 --> 00:54:20,741
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why nothing can come in between it.

574
00:54:20,781 --> 00:54:22,942
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, see, that's the thing.

575
00:54:23,562 --> 00:54:36,128
[SPEAKER_03]: If craving, if freedom from craving, freedom from ignorance, all these things, actually required time, or had anything to do with time, once you achieved it, there will be no guarantee that it won't come back.

576
00:54:36,288 --> 00:54:40,790
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, once you achieve the freedom from it, there will be no guarantee that it won't come back.

577
00:54:40,850 --> 00:54:45,052
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it meant that it was basically like external to you, imposed on you.

578
00:54:45,916 --> 00:54:49,458
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, if you were free from it, that would be basically circumstantial.

579
00:54:49,558 --> 00:54:50,978
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if the last...

580
00:54:51,038 --> 00:54:52,519
[SPEAKER_00]: It was something that happened to you.

581
00:54:52,539 --> 00:54:53,259
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

582
00:54:53,339 --> 00:54:53,679
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

583
00:54:53,699 --> 00:55:00,442
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's something that happened to you, something that just came to sort of replace the stuff that was there before.

584
00:55:01,003 --> 00:55:03,384
[SPEAKER_02]: But no, because it is fully based on understanding.

585
00:55:04,724 --> 00:55:13,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, can you forget something when you were assuming something, and then you know the actual nature of it?

586
00:55:14,746 --> 00:55:15,807
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you ever forget?

587
00:55:16,868 --> 00:55:18,289
[SPEAKER_02]: You can forget specific things.

588
00:55:18,329 --> 00:55:24,475
[SPEAKER_02]: You can forget, you know, where a certain place lies in a map or something.

589
00:55:24,615 --> 00:55:31,161
[SPEAKER_02]: But that fundamental knowledge is something that, well, it pertains to the nature of your very experience.

590
00:55:31,201 --> 00:55:35,525
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for as long as experience is there, you won't be able to forget it.

591
00:55:35,585 --> 00:55:37,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Because every single phenomenon

592
00:55:38,828 --> 00:55:40,769
[SPEAKER_02]: involves that same nature.

593
00:55:40,829 --> 00:55:46,072
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you basically... it's impossible for you to forget, for as long as you're alive.

594
00:55:46,633 --> 00:55:53,536
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's in opposition to something that just happens.

595
00:55:53,857 --> 00:56:04,963
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if it's supposedly through your effort and your years of practice, the fact that you still thought of it as, okay, yeah, time after this 20 years,

596
00:56:06,451 --> 00:56:17,779
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you still have the view, no, all of this effort was necessary to bring me to this point, because it was like loading up this... this... this meter or something.

597
00:56:18,679 --> 00:56:20,320
[SPEAKER_02]: That means you don't see it on the right level.

598
00:56:20,440 --> 00:56:24,463
[SPEAKER_02]: You think it... you're basically just dealing with circumstances.

599
00:56:27,625 --> 00:56:31,488
[SPEAKER_03]: It should be like, when you're free from it, you see

600
00:56:32,712 --> 00:56:37,754
[SPEAKER_03]: If, if back, if from day one I would have known this, I would have been free from day one.

601
00:56:38,454 --> 00:56:49,998
[SPEAKER_03]: That time was, strictly speaking, it was like necessary, because ignorance is beginningless, but it's not intrinsic to the process of purification.

602
00:56:50,499 --> 00:56:55,761
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, Bahiya and stuff, you know, he went from Putrajana to Arahant in basically a moment.

603
00:56:58,381 --> 00:57:02,243
[SPEAKER_01]: I was... maybe you could even get a glimpse of that on the level of, um...

604
00:57:04,039 --> 00:57:07,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Bring it back down to, like, even just the level of keeping the precepts again.

605
00:57:08,101 --> 00:57:17,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, keeping the precepts, in a way, I think it can often sound like, okay, yeah, this is something you now have to, like, do and take on.

606
00:57:17,785 --> 00:57:25,868
[SPEAKER_01]: But even somebody who is completely unrestrained, like, do they spend... do you spend more time breaking the precepts or not breaking the precepts?

607
00:57:26,789 --> 00:57:29,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, actually, I spend much more time not breaking the precepts.

608
00:57:30,353 --> 00:57:38,437
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all I have to do is, on certain very specific occasions, not make choices that I would have made in the past.

609
00:57:39,238 --> 00:57:49,583
[SPEAKER_01]: And I realize I find myself already in a state that was kind of already there under my feet, in a way.

610
00:57:50,044 --> 00:57:52,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I was just...

611
00:57:53,485 --> 00:57:57,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in it, because of, kind of, going out of your way, in a way.

612
00:57:57,288 --> 00:58:05,353
[SPEAKER_01]: And I suppose, like, for some, from the point of view of somebody who is habitually breaking the precepts and not keeping the virtue, that is what, kind of, feels like the natural state.

613
00:58:05,373 --> 00:58:07,235
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels automatic, feels effortless.

614
00:58:07,635 --> 00:58:12,918
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize, actually, well, which, which, kind of, is more complicated, basically?

615
00:58:13,259 --> 00:58:19,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it more complicated to, like, you know, go out of my way, tell, you know, misrepresent the truth, tell a lie?

616
00:58:20,764 --> 00:58:22,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it more complicated to have to, you know,

617
00:58:26,574 --> 00:58:27,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

618
00:58:27,814 --> 00:58:32,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Steal something from somebody else, kill, whatever.

619
00:58:33,576 --> 00:58:37,357
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more complicated, to put it in that way.

620
00:58:37,977 --> 00:58:41,238
[SPEAKER_01]: It involves more effort than not doing these things.

621
00:58:42,238 --> 00:58:51,060
[SPEAKER_01]: And you were already spending more time, having more of your life in the state of not

622
00:58:52,079 --> 00:59:11,110
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's more like just to kind of... well, you would realize, I suppose, once you've developed this, that you actually were just developing something that was already kind of there in a nascent form, and that was... that you could have accessed, that you could have always accessed.

623
00:59:11,230 --> 00:59:14,132
[SPEAKER_01]: It was not something that you have to go out and like seek and gain.

624
00:59:15,613 --> 00:59:20,976
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not like a... like let's say, you know, a pilot, like factually.

625
00:59:22,083 --> 00:59:33,125
[SPEAKER_03]: It is impossible that someone has never driven a plane before, and he just gets in, somebody tells him something, and he does it perfectly.

626
00:59:33,185 --> 00:59:34,385
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's a mundane skill.

627
00:59:35,246 --> 00:59:38,346
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why treating the dhamma as a skill is extremely misguided.

628
00:59:38,406 --> 00:59:40,527
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like... no, it's from day one.

629
00:59:41,207 --> 00:59:47,868
[SPEAKER_03]: If you were... if your own, as the Buddha said, dust in your eyes was not there, this would have been obvious.

630
00:59:50,069 --> 00:59:53,250
[SPEAKER_03]: The time really, you could say, that's the best way to put it.

631
00:59:53,270 --> 00:59:56,472
[SPEAKER_03]: The time, seeing it, didn't require time.

632
00:59:57,412 --> 01:00:02,335
[SPEAKER_02]: What required time was taking off the dust in your eyes that prevented you from seeing what was already there the whole time.

